; it-patterns-masks — the pattern stream's own corners.
;
; Channel 0 repeats one cell four rows running, so its mask is sent once
; and then reused (a bare lead byte per row). Channel 1 walks every
; family of the volume column — volume, the five slide families, pan,
; tone porta, vibrato — as IT's raw bytes. Channels 2–6 carry the command
; bytes 27–31, which sit inside ITTECH's range and name no effect: the
; reader keeps the cell, drops the command and says so
; (`it_command_unknown`).
;
; This is one of the corpus's two NON-CANONICAL fixtures, and it is here
; for rung 2. Those five commands read as NO effect, so writing the
; module back produces a legitimately SHORTER stream and `(rung1 :ok
; false)` is the expected, PINNED fact — rung 1 is a gate on files the
; canonical writer produced from a module it can read back, and this file
; is authored to fail that premise. Rung 2 is what the case asserts: the
; header, the orders, every pattern cell and every pass-through region
; survive the round trip, so what the writer dropped is exactly the five
; commands the reader had already dropped and nothing else. A golden is
; reviewed, not obeyed: byte-equality of this whole text with
; `expected.sjon` is the gate.
;
; `(write)` beside it hashes the normalised module, so the mask cache,
; every volume byte and the five dropped commands are pinned by a number
; that moves if any of them does.
(read)
(roundtrip)
(write)
